Scripts placed here are run when wansentry's ACTIVE UPLINK CHANGES, and at no
other time. Each must be executable. See /etc/hotplug.d/iface/99-wansentry for
the full contract; the short version is:

  WANSENTRY_OLD     uplink that was carrying traffic, or "none"
  WANSENTRY_NEW     uplink now carrying traffic, or "none"
  WANSENTRY_EVENT   the netifd action that triggered the check

A hook that exits non-zero is logged and ignored. Hooks run as root.

Nothing here is required. The directory ships with only this file, and the
hotplug script exits immediately when it finds nothing executable.

Example, reload a service that caches its egress interface:

    cat > /etc/wansentry.d/10-reload-ddns <<'SH'
    #!/bin/sh
    [ "$WANSENTRY_NEW" = "none" ] && exit 0
    /etc/init.d/ddns reload
    SH
    chmod +x /etc/wansentry.d/10-reload-ddns

Before writing a hook, check whether you need one. Measured on OpenVPN 2.7, a
tunnel follows a switchover by itself without restarting, because the session is
keyed on a negotiated peer-id rather than on the client's source address.
Restarting it would be a disruption with nothing to fix. Measure your own case
before automating a reaction to it.
